{{smaller block|<poem>Force ''vegetiue'' and sensatiue in Man
There is. ''Heywood'', Hierarchy of Angels, p. 13.</poem>}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' A vegetable.
{{smaller block|<poem>Make us better than those ''vegetives''
Whose souls die with them.</poem>
{{right|''Middleton, Massinger, and Rowley'', Old Law, i. 1.}}}}

'''vegeto-alkali''' (vej″ẹ̄-tō-al′ka-li), ''n.'' An alkaloid.

'''vegeto-animal''' (vej″ẹ̄-tō-an′i-mal), ''a.'' and ''n.'' '''I.''' ''a.'' Partaking of the nature of both vegetable and animal matter.—'''Vegeto-animal matter''', {{smaller|a name formerly applied to vegetable gluten and albumen.}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' An organism of equivocal character between a plant and an animal; a protist.

