strength, < ''vehemen(t-)s'', eager: see ''vehement.''] The character or state of being vehement; the energy exhibited by one who or that which is vehement. {{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') Violent ardor; fervor; impetuosity; fire: as, the ''vehemence'' of love or affection; the ''vehemence'' of anger or other passion.}}
{{smaller block|Nay, I prithee now with most petitionary ''vehemence'', tell me who it is.
{{right|''Shak.'', As you Like it, iii. 2. 200.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') Force or impetuosity accompanying energetic action of any kind; impetuous force; impetuosity; boisterousness; violence; fury: as, the ''vehemence'' of wind; to speak with ''vehemence.''}}
{{smaller block|<poem>A universal hubbub wild
Of stunning sounds and voices all confused,
Borne through the hollow dark, assaults his ear
With loudest ''vehemence.''</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., ii. 954.}}}}
='''Syn.''' {{smaller|Force, might, intensity, passion.}}

'''vehemency''' (vē′hē-men-si), ''n.'' [As ''vehemence'' (see ''-cy'').] Same as ''vehemence.''
{{smaller block|<poem>The ''vehemency'' of this passion’s such,
Many have died by joying overmuch.</poem>
{{right|''Times’ Whistle'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 94.}}}}

'''vehement''' (vē′hē-ment), ''a.'' [< OF. ''vehement'', F. ''véhément'' = Sp. Pg. ''vehemente'' = It. ''veemente'', < L. ''vehemen(t-)s'', sometimes contr. ''veemen(t-)s'', ''vēmen(t-)s'', very eager, impetuous, ardent, furious, appar. < ''vehere'', carry (or *''veha'', ''vea'', ''via'', way?), + ''men(t-)s'', mind: see ''vehicle'' and ''mental''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' Proceeding from or characterized by strength, violence, or impetuosity of feeling or emotion; very ardent; very eager or urgent; fervent; passionate.
{{smaller block|<poem>Note, if your lady strain his entertainment
With any strong or ''vehement'' importunity.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Othello, iii. 3. 251.}}}}
{{smaller block|I fell into some ''vehement'' argumentations with him in}}

